{{~##
 What every generated header starts with: the banner, the include guard, the includes it
 needs, and the `extern "C"` block when it declares anything with linkage.

 C is the one target where this is not bookkeeping. An include has to come before the thing
 that uses it, a struct member of struct type needs the complete type, and a header included
 twice in one translation unit has to be harmless. All three are answered here, from what
 TypeDependencies says the file names.

 `extern "C"` only where it means something. A typedef, an enum and a struct have no
 linkage, so an enum header does not need it; a function declaration and an `extern const`
 do.

 This always ends on a blank line, whichever of those parts it wrote, so the file's first
 declaration is never up against the guard or the `extern "C"` open. The foot leaves the
 same gap ahead of the closes.
##~}}
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Generated by Tabbit. DO NOT EDIT.
 *
 * Changes to this file may cause incorrect behavior and will be lost if the code is
 * regenerated.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

#ifndef {{ guard }}
#define {{ guard }}
{{~ if includes | array.size > 0 }}
{{~ for line in includes ~}}
{{ line }}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if extern_c }}
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif
{{~ end ~}}

